      "content": "You raise an excellent and crucial point that forensic linguists discuss constantly regarding racial and socioeconomic bias. While certain linguistic features in Transcript B (like the phonetic spelling and filler words) often statistically correlate with unimpressive educational backgrounds rather than explicitly lying, juries possess a natural tendency toward linguistic prejudice. Research suggests that non-standard grammar can inadvertently trigger a \"pseudo-intelligence\" bias or a presumption of corruption, effectively penalizing the witness unfairly regardless of their actual honesty. This social phenomenon highlights a significant challenge in forensic linguistics: distinguishing between genuine markers of memory impairment and the unconscious projection of stereotypes by fact-finders. Ultimately, while the text in Transcript A *appears* more credible because it aligns with dominant cultural norms of testimony, determining true reliability requires objectively verifying facts rather than relying on linguistic style.",
